--- !u!114 &213903423
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 213903422}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: d1f52624f7848bc42864aa0896c08652, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  dialogueBox: {fileID: 1437676594}
  dialogueText: {fileID: 293847348}
  questionBox: {fileID: 1437676594}
  questionText: {fileID: 293847348}
  answerButtons:
  - {fileID: 1531244610}
  - {fileID: 78181992}
  - {fileID: 846034147}
  answerButtonTexts:
  - {fileID: 1108707437}
  - {fileID: 1873611403}
  - {fileID: 1879969926}
  allVisualGroups:
  - {fileID: 531628974}
  - {fileID: 1847045269}
  - {fileID: 568742454}
  - {fileID: 438219105}
  - {fileID: 68723881}
  - {fileID: 1301033546}
  nextButton: {fileID: 1015302479}
  questions:
  - visualGroup: {fileID: 531628974}
    teachingLines:
    - "Night falls over the Day of the Dead festival.\nDaniel stands beneath Mariana\u2019s
      balcony \nwith his face painted like a skull. Candles, \nmarigolds, and festival
      lights glow around him."
    - "The paint hides his fear, but the song \nmust show his heart. This is his
      chance \nto say what he could never say out loud."
    - "In this final level, you will help Daniel complete \nthe serenade. Use notes,
      rests, chords, and \nsong sections to build the full song."
    - "Each measure must still follow the rules of 4/4 \ntime. Choose the best intro,
      verses, chorus,and\noutro so the song can be played from the heart."
    - Let's Begin!
    - "The intro begins the song.\r\nFor this serenade, the intro should \nuse notes
      only and total 4 beats."
    questionText: Which measure works best for the intro?
    answerA: C Major R R
    answerB: C D E G
    answerC: C R C Major
    correctAnswerIndex: 1
    secondCorrectAnswerIndex: -1
    correctFeedbackLines:
    - "Correct!\nThe intro uses notes only, \nand C D E G adds up to 4 beats."
    incorrectFeedbackLines:
    - "Not quite.\nThe intro should use notes \nonly and still add up to 4 beats.\nTry
      again."
  - visualGroup: {fileID: 1847045269}
    teachingLines:
    - "Verse 1 begins the main musical idea.\r\nFor this section, use at least one
      note, \nand at least one chord."
    questionText: Which measure works best for Verse 1?
    answerA: C D E G
    answerB: C Major A minor
    answerC: C R C Major
    correctAnswerIndex: 2
    secondCorrectAnswerIndex: -1
    correctFeedbackLines:
    - "Correct!\nC R C Major uses a note, a rest, \nand a chord. It also adds up
      to 4 beats."
    incorrectFeedbackLines:
    - "Not quite.\nVerse 1 should use at least one note, \nand at least one chord.
      \nTry again."
  - visualGroup: {fileID: 568742454}
    teachingLines:
    - "The chorus is the strongest part of \nthe song. For this section, use a chord
      \nand rests while still totaling 4 beats."
    questionText: Which measure works best for the chorus?
    answerA: C Major R A Major
    answerB: C Major C D E
    answerC: C R G R
    correctAnswerIndex: 0
    secondCorrectAnswerIndex: -1
    correctFeedbackLines:
    - "Correct!\nC Major R R uses a chord and \nrests, and it adds up to 4 beats."
    incorrectFeedbackLines:
    - "Not quite.\nThe chorus should include a \nchord and still add up to 4 beats.\nTry
      again."
  - visualGroup: {fileID: 438219105}
    teachingLines:
    - "Verse 2 brings back the musical idea \nwith more confidence.Like Verse 1,
      \nthis section should use one note, \none rest, and one chord."
    questionText: Which measure works best for Verse 2?
    answerA: A minor R R
    answerB: E R A minor
    answerC: C D E G
    correctAnswerIndex: 1
    secondCorrectAnswerIndex: -1
    correctFeedbackLines:
    - "Correct!\nE R A minor uses a note, \na rest, and a chord."
    incorrectFeedbackLines:
    - "Not quite.\nVerse 2 should use one note, \none rest, and one chord.\nTry again."
  - visualGroup: {fileID: 68723881}
    teachingLines:
    - "The outro ends the serenade.\r\nFor this final section, use notes \nand end
      on a final chord."
    questionText: Which measure works best for the outro?
    answerA: C Major R R
    answerB: C D E G
    answerC: G E C Major
    correctAnswerIndex: 2
    secondCorrectAnswerIndex: -1
    correctFeedbackLines:
    - "Correct!\nG E C Major uses notes \nand ends on a final chord."
    incorrectFeedbackLines:
    - "Not quite.\nThe outro should end \non a final chord.\nTry again."
  - visualGroup: {fileID: 1301033546}
    teachingLines:
    - 'Congratulations!

      You completed the serenade.'
    - 'The notes are written.

      The rests are placed.

      The chords are
      ready.'
    - "He looks up at the balcony.\r\nFor the first time, \nthe song says what he
      \ncould not say out loud."
    - "Now there is only \none thing left to do..."
    questionText: Are you brave enough to play it?
    answerA: YES
    answerB: Not yet...
    answerC: I'm not sure...
    correctAnswerIndex: 0
    secondCorrectAnswerIndex: -1
    correctFeedbackLines:
    - 'He lifts the guitar into place.

      His fingers hover over the strings.

      The
      balcony waits above him.

      He takes one final breath.'
    - 'He does not know what her answer will be.

      He does not know if she will
      understand.

      Maybe she will smile, maybe she won''t.

      Maybe he
      will never know.'
    - 'But the song is real.

      Every note carries what he could not say.

      The
      answer is left somewhere beyond the silence.

      Finding the courage to
      play is enough.'
    incorrectFeedbackLines:
    - 'The song is finished.

      Now he has to find the courage to play it.

      Come
      back when you''re ready.'
  typeSpeed: 0.04
